---
/**
 * /supply-chain/ — the manufacturer side, published 2026-09-11.
 *
 * D29 DEFERRED THIS IN ITS OWN WORDS — "secondary, or a later venture as the
 * practice grows" — so this page is that deferral ending rather than a reversal of
 * it. The operator's answer in the alignment session was to build it properly with
 * a page of its own rather than as a framing sentence on an existing one.
 *
 * NO PUBLISHED FIGURE, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING. Every deliverable is quoted per
 * engagement exactly as projectModel is, so this adds nothing to the count of
 * published prices and D93's standing warning about the next figure is untouched.
 * If a number ever appears on this page, the warning applies and the test in
 * src/lib/pricing.js has to be run and written down first.
 *
 * FIXED PRICE BY DELIVERABLE, NEVER A DAY RATE, AND THE REASON IS ARITHMETIC.
 * Day-rate work in this field is weekday-business-hours work, and
 * src/lib/consult-hours.js publishes weekday evenings and weekend mornings —
 * fifteen hours a week, none of it inside a business day. A day rate would be
 * selling hours that do not exist. THIS IS THE CONSTRAINT THE ALIGNMENT SESSION
 * FOUND APPLIED HERE AND NOT TO ADVISORY: the same approved plan named this file
 * by name for this offer, then shipped a product promising a reply on a working
 * day. Advisory was repaired on 2026-09-11; this page was written with it from the
 * start, and the page says so to the reader rather than only to the next editor.
 *
 * THE EMPLOYER IS NEVER NAMED AND NEITHER IS ANY PLANT. D1 and D8 hold without
 * exception. D86 means scripts/verify-industrial.mjs reads this page's RENDERED
 * text for stray plant codes, and body prose is the easiest place to introduce one
 * — so no string on this page or in `supplyChain` carries a bare four-digit number.
 *
 * THE PROOF IS DERIVED, NEVER LISTED. The at-scale demos are read out of
 * workGroups by band, which is the same filter /work/ and /llms.txt already use, so
 * a new at-scale group joins this page for free and no count is typed anywhere
 * (D22). They were proof of caliber with nothing stated under them; this is the
 * stated thing they are proof OF.
 *
 * NO PAY BUTTON AND NO BOOKING LINK. This starts with somebody describing a number
 * they do not trust, which is a conversation rather than a checkout.
 */
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import { supplyChain } from '../data/services';
/* BOTH COUNTS ON THIS PAGE DERIVE SINCE 2026-09-13, AND ONE OF THEM WAS TWO
   SUBTRACTIONS AWAY FROM ITS SOURCE. "five other industries" is the career list
   minus the beats that are the current plant work; it was typed, and correct
   only because nobody had added a trade since. Insurance became the seventh beat
   that same day. */
import { countWord } from '../lib/count-words.js';
import { priorTradeCountWord } from '../lib/trades.js';
import { workGroups, demos } from '../data/demos';
import { Boxes, Ruler, ArrowUpRight } from '@lucide/astro';

const origin = new URL(Astro.site!).origin;

/* DERIVED, NEVER TYPED — the same filter /work/index.astro and /llms.txt.ts use.
   A group joining the at-scale band appears here with no edit to this file. */
const scaleGroups = workGroups.filter((g) => g.band === 'scale');
const bySlug = new Map(demos.map((d) => [d.slug, d]));

const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'Service',
    '@id': `${origin}/supply-chain/#service`,
    name: 'Supply-chain and master-data audits for manufacturers',
    serviceType: 'Supply chain consulting',
    provider: { '@id': `${origin}/#business` },
    description:
      'Fixed-price audits of bill-of-materials accuracy, planning parameters against real consumption, and change-order cutover timing. Quoted per engagement.',
  },
];
---

<MarketingLayout
  title="Numbers your planning system is sure about"
  description="Fixed-price audits for manufacturers: bill-of-materials accuracy, planning parameters against real consumption, and when a change can actually be implemented."
  schema={schema}
>
  {/* ── Hero ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      D113 CARRIED OFF THE HOME PAGE, 2026-09-13. This page ran four bands at
      max-w-6xl, 6xl, 5xl and 6xl with one vertical rhythm, and the only thing
      that ever changed underneath them was which two carried the surface
      ground — canvas, surface, canvas, surface, alternating on a fixed beat.
      The at-scale pitch is the longest single argument on this site and it read
      as one column that would not stop.

      It now walks feature, content, popout, feature for the column and canvas,
      surface, deep, canvas for the ground, with the rhythm tightening under the
      last of them. No two neighbors share more than one heavy signal, and the
      palette does none of the work — a hue is the lightest signal on a page,
      which is the argument src/components/kit/industrial.js makes under WHY
      THREE AND NOT SEVEN.

      THE COLUMN NARROWS INTO THE TERMS AND OPENS BACK OUT FOR THE PROOF, which
      is the page's own argument in shape: the claim gets the wide measure, the
      three things you can buy get a reading column, the price and the refusal
      get the narrowest one on the page, and the demos get the widest because a
      gallery is the one thing here a reader scans rather than reads. */}
  <Section width="feature" class="border-b border-line">
    <Fragment slot="backdrop">
      <div class="bg-grid bg-grid-fade pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 opacity-60"></div>
    </Fragment>
      <p class="mono-label">For manufacturers · fixed price per answer · never a day rate</p>
      <h1 class="statement mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        The numbers your planning system is sure about.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        {supplyChain.whoItIsFor}
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        I have spent the last few years on a planning floor, and before that in operations in {priorTradeCountWord()}
        other industries. The work below is the work I do on a weekday: not advice about it.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        {/* `btn` CARRIES THE SHAPE AND `btn-primary` ONLY THE PAINT, and the
            strongest call to action on this page shipped without the first of
            them. global.css:474 holds the padding, the inline-flex row and the
            corner radius; :495 adds the gradient and the glow on top. Alone,
            the second paints a hard-edged rectangle with the label pressed
            against its sides and the arrow wrapped onto a second line. Same
            defect, same repair, as the one found on /quoted/ on 2026-09-13 —
            nothing here measures a control's geometry, and the color it paints
            was never the part that was wrong. */}
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact/?intent=consult">
          Tell me which number you do not trust <ArrowUpRight class="size-4" />
        </a>
        <a class="link-sweep text-sm" href="/work/">Or see what I have built at that scale →</a>
      </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ── The three deliverables ───────────────────────────────────────────────
      The surface ground and the reading column are both against the hero: the
      claim gets the wide measure, the things that earn it get a column a reader
      holds and a ground of their own. The numeral above the label is gone —
      PICKUP.md scores a decorative "/ 01" as a generated-design signal, and
      these were never a sequence: nothing stops a reader arriving at the proof
      band without having read this one. */}
  <Section tone="surface" width="content">
      <p class="mono-label">What you can buy</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-2xl font-display text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink sm:text-4xl">
        {countWord(supplyChain.deliverables.length, { capital: true })} answers, each one finished.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Each of these ends. You get a written answer you can act on or hand to somebody else, and
        the price is agreed before it starts.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
        {supplyChain.deliverables.map((d) => (
          <div class="panel lift spotlight flex flex-col gap-3 p-6">
            <span class="grid h-11 w-11 place-items-center rounded-lg border border-line bg-canvas text-accent-2">
              <Ruler size={20} aria-hidden="true" />
            </span>
            <h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold text-ink">{d.title}</h3>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{d.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ── How it is sold, and the refusal ─────────────────────────────────────
      THE DEEP GROUND IS SPENT HERE ON PURPOSE, and it is the heaviest signal
      this page has. This is the only band that states terms rather than
      offering something — the fixed price, the reason there is no day rate, and
      the refusal to take this work from appliance manufacturers — so the page
      stopping is what the ground should say. `deep` and not `instrument`: both
      panels are sized for a light ground, which is the reading /work/ and
      /websites/ already wrote down when they declined the dark chassis.

      THE NARROWEST COLUMN ON THE PAGE, AND THE STANDARD RHYTHM UNDER IT. Two
      panels of terms do not want the measure the grid above them needs, and
      pulling them in is what stops this reading as one more row of cards. It
      was written with `tall` as well and the capture taken for this pass is why
      it is not: the makeover document warns that `tall` on a band already
      carrying something tall pays twice, and this is the mirror of that — the
      shortest content on the page inside the narrowest column, with the longest
      rhythm around it, left two panels floating in an empty field. Ground and
      column are two heavy signals and they are enough. */}
  <Section tone="deep" width="popout">
    <div class="grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="panel flex flex-col gap-3 p-7">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <Boxes class="size-5 text-accent" />
          <span class="mono-label">How it is priced</span>
        </div>
        <p class="font-display text-lg font-semibold leading-snug text-ink">
          A fixed price for a finished answer.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{supplyChain.howItIsSold}</p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel flex flex-col gap-3 p-7">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <Ruler class="size-5 text-secondary" />
          <span class="mono-label">If you want a tool instead</span>
        </div>
        <p class="font-display text-lg font-semibold leading-snug text-ink">
          Scoped when you buy it, and yours outright.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{supplyChain.toolHalf}</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">{supplyChain.conflictRule}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ── The proof, derived from the at-scale bands ───────────────────────────
      Back to the page's own ground, out to the widest column, and tight under
      the band above. Three signals again, and each one is doing a job: a
      gallery of demos is scanned rather than read, so it wants the room the
      terms band deliberately gave up, and the standard rhythm stacked on top of
      several rows of cards read as a gap rather than as air — the same reading
      /main-street/ wrote down over its screenshot.

      THIS IS THE LAST BAND, AND IT ENDS ON CANVAS DELIBERATELY. The page has
      just spent its heaviest ground on the refusal; carrying a second painted
      ground past it would make the terms band one of a pair rather than the one
      place the page stops. */}
  <Section width="feature" density="tight">
      <p class="mono-label">Built, running, and openable</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-2xl font-display text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink sm:text-4xl">
        This is not a proposal. Open one.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Each of these runs in your browser with nothing to install and no account to make. They use
        invented data from a fictional manufacturer, and the arithmetic in them is the arithmetic I
        would bring to yours.
      </p>

      {scaleGroups.map((g) => (
        <div class="mt-9">
          <p class="mono-label text-cyan-deep">{g.label}</p>
          <div class="mt-3 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
            {g.slugs.map((slug) => {
              const d = bySlug.get(slug);
              return d ? (
                <a href={`/work/${slug}/`} class="panel lift spotlight flex flex-col gap-2 p-5">
                  <h3 class="font-display text-base font-semibold text-ink">{d.name}</h3>
                  <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">{d.blurb}</p>
                  <span class="link-sweep mt-1 text-sm">Open it →</span>
                </a>
              ) : null;
            })}
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
  </Section>
</MarketingLayout>
